This is not accurate.
Replay logic does NOT “enter L0”.
Replay happens only in L0 state.
Important rule:
👉 TLP transmission is allowed only in L0
👉 No TLP transmission in L0s, L1, L2
________________________________________
✅ Correct Behaviour (Very Important)
🔹 What is L0s?
L0s is a standby state:
•	Transmitter electrical idle
•	Fast exit possible
•	Used for power saving
But:
⚠️ No packet transmission allowed in L0s.
________________________________________
🔹 So What Happens When Replay Timer Expires in L0s?
When Replay Timer expires:
Transmitter wants to retransmit.
But link is in L0s.
So:
1.	LTSSM automatically initiates Exit from L0s → back to L0
2.	Once link returns to L0
3.	Then Data Link Layer performs replay
Replay never happens inside L0s.
________________________________________
🔥 Important Architecture Concept
LTSSM controls physical link state.
Data Link Layer depends on LTSSM.
If link is not in L0:
→ DLL transmission is blocked.
________________________________________
💡 Clean Interview Answer
If interviewer asks:
What happens if Replay Timer expires in L0s?
You should answer:
•	Replay requires active link (L0 state)
•	If link is in L0s, LTSSM first transitions link back to L0
•	After link becomes active, DLL initiates replay
•	No TLP transmission occurs in L0s
